TEXT 144 32 Left 3 ;#387 20 m trap, measured as a series element
TEXT 144 96 Left 2 ;Source: Alan Wolke (W2AEW), https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KIEZmwB8AHw
TEXT 144 144 Left 2 ;Ltrap and Ctrap in parallel, the pair in series with a 50 ohm through path. At resonance the
TEXT 144 176 Left 2 ;pair is a high impedance and almost nothing reaches the load, which is the notch Alan tunes for.
TEXT 144 624 Left 2 ;The kit uses a T50-2 core and a 15 pF capacitor. The inductance is whatever the winding
TEXT 144 656 Left 2 ;comes out at, and Alan adjusts it while watching a NanoVNA, so neither the kit listing nor the
TEXT 144 688 Left 2 ;video fixes it. 8.3748uH here is what the capacitor needs for resonance at 14.2 MHz.
TEXT 144 736 Left 2 ;Rser=5 is a Q of 150 at resonance, which is plausible for a hand-wound T50-2 trap but is an
TEXT 144 768 Left 2 ;assumption, not a measurement. It is what sets how deep the notch goes: a lossless coil would
TEXT 144 800 Left 2 ;give an infinitely deep one. The frequency is the result that does not depend on it.
TEXT 144 848 Left 2 ;The fixture is a 50 ohm source and a 50 ohm load, which is what a NanoVNA presents when it
TEXT 144 880 Left 2 ;measures through a part. In the antenna the trap sits in series with the wire instead.
TEXT 800 400 Left 2 !.ac dec 2000 5Meg 25Meg
TEXT 800 440 Left 2 !.meas ac vmin MIN mag(V(trap_out))
TEXT 800 480 Left 2 !.meas ac v_res FIND mag(V(trap_out)) AT 14.2Meg
TEXT 800 520 Left 2 !.meas ac v_edge FIND mag(V(trap_out)) AT 14.35Meg
